// mecatl driver API: the remote event-log protocol.
//
// EventLogService is the wire seam behind the harness's `port.EventLog`
// (engine/port/eventlog.go): an operator-run driver process persists the
// append-only, per-session DURABLE record of a run's event stream — the rich
// timeline (live reasoning, ask/verdict pairs, delegation lifecycle) the
// gRPC/HTTP relay otherwise emits and discards — in place of the in-process
// jsonlstore. The driver sits at the SAME trust tier as an on-disk store
// directory: operator-configured infrastructure, not a model-facing surface.
//
// The event payload is OPAQUE to the driver: it is exactly the harness's
// per-event JSON encoding, identified by the envelope's `format` tag
// ("eventlog-json/1" today; see internal/adapter/grpcdriver.EventLogFormat,
// the SAME tag the local jsonlstore writes). The driver stores and returns the
// bytes VERBATIM and never decodes them — event schema evolution lives in
// session.Event (additive JSON), while format identification lives in this
// envelope. This mirrors how the session snapshot crosses on
// SessionStoreService: opaque, format-tagged bytes, decoded harness-side only.
//
// The log records ALREADY-REDACTED events (the relay is the redaction
// boundary — Subagent/Parallel payloads metadata-only, Team previews capped,
// surfaced child asks clamped; gauntlet #7), so the driver adds no redaction.
//
// Driver contract (mirrors port.EventLog):
//   - Append durably records one event under a session id, in raw APPEND order.
//     A non-OK status means "not recorded": the harness relay logs a WARN and
//     never aborts the live run, but it relies on OK meaning durable (a backend
//     that cannot guarantee durability before responding MUST return an error,
//     not buffer silently). The caller appends each event AT MOST ONCE and
//     never retries, so a driver need NOT deduplicate.
//   - Read STREAMS the session's recorded events in the exact order Append
//     received them — implementations MUST NOT reorder. A session id that was
//     never appended to yields an EMPTY stream (absence is data, never
//     NOT_FOUND), exactly like an empty conversation. A mid-stream
//     infrastructure fault aborts the stream with a non-OK status.
//
// Read is SERVER-STREAMING (unlike every other driver RPC, which is unary): a
// run's log grows unbounded, so a unary Read would eventually hit the 64 MiB
// message cap, and the stream maps 1:1 onto port.EventLog.Read's lazy
// iter.Seq2.
//
// Validation: required-field annotations are authored with `buf.validate.field`
// for documentation and future runtime enforcement; V1 enforces them in the Go
// client/server wrappers (internal/adapter/grpcdriver).

